Q: CSS native variables not working in media queries A: <p>With modern CSS, you can simulate media queries using if() and calculation. You will then be able to adjust the breakpoint easily (more detail on my blog: <a href="https://css-tip.com/dynamic-queries/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://css-tip.com/dynamic-queries/</a>)</p>
<p>Here is a basic example:</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-css lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>:root {
  --breakpoint: 500px;
}

.container {
  --g: max(0,sign(100vw - var(--breakpoint)));
  /* lightblue if screen_width &gt; breakpoint 
     darkred   if screen_width &lt; breakpoint 
  */
  background: if(style(--g = 1): lightblue; else: darkred);

  min-height: 200px;
}</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;div class="container"&gt;&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>

Q: Can an ES6 class be made callable without using the “new” keyword? A: <p>No, this is not possible. Constructors that are created using the <code>class</code> keyword can only be constructed with <code>new</code>, if they are <a href="https://people.mozilla.org/%7Ejorendorff/es6-draft.html#sec-ecmascript-function-objects-call-thisargument-argumentslist" rel="nofollow noreferrer">[[call]]ed</a> without they always <code>throw</code> a <code>TypeError</code><sup>1</sup> (and there's not even a way to detect this from the outside).<br />
<sub>1: I'm not sure whether transpilers get this right</sub></p>
<p>You can use a normal function as a workaround, though:</p>
<pre><code>class Foo {
  constructor(x) {
    this.x = x;
  }
  hello() {
    return `hello ${this.x}`;
  }
}
{
  const _Foo = Foo;
  Foo = function(...args) {
    return new _Foo(...args);
  };
  Foo.prototype = _Foo.prototype;
}
</code></pre>
<p><sub>Disclaimer: <code>instanceof</code> and extending <code>Foo.prototype</code> work as normal, <code>Foo.length</code> does not, <code>.constructor</code> and static methods do not but can be fixed by adding <code>Foo.prototype.constructor = Foo;</code> and <code>Object.setPrototypeOf(Foo, _Foo)</code> if required.</sub></p>
<p>To support subclassing <code>Foo</code> (not <code>_Foo</code>) with <code>class Bar extends Foo …</code>, you should use <code>return Reflect.construct(_Foo, args, new.target)</code> instead of the <code>new _Foo</code> call. Subclassing in ES5 style (with <code>Foo.call(this, …)</code>) is not possible.</p>
